{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nFound while proving `peckish-marmatite-cuckoo` (`db2c-3a4b-1826-451f`) AC5, which requires\nthat deleting each `monkeypatch.setattr` on a resolver name makes its test FAIL.\n\nTwo of the 5 patch-site modules do not satisfy that oracle, and **the weakness predates\ndb2c**. Re-measured on `origin/main` `73e90b296e2` (after `374cc2f22b1` repointed both\npatches from the shim to `rebar._ids`): deleting the patch still leaves each test green.\n\n- `tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py:409` —\n  `monkeypatch.setattr(resolver, \"build_resolver_scan_index\", lambda _tracker: None)`\n  (`resolver` is `rebar._ids`), test\n  `test_referencing_commits_falls_back_to_per_call_scan_without_index`.\n  Deleting it: `1 passed`.\n- `tests/unit/test_completion_sidecar_material_balding.py:227-230` —\n  `monkeypatch.setattr(\"rebar._ids.resolve_ticket_dir_name\", ...)`, test\n  `test_legacy_payloads_without_material_still_round_trip`. Deleting it: `1 passed`.\n\n## Root cause (from the production code, not the symptom)\n\n1. **attach_commits.** `commit_impact.referencing_commits` (`commit_impact.py:287-310`)\n   builds the scan index and passes `alias_index` / `dir_names` to `resolve_ticket_id`;\n   when the index is `None` it passes `None` for both. The test asserts only that the\n   target SHA is credited, which both paths do by design (\"The result is identical to\n   per-call scanning\", `_ids.resolve_ticket_id` docstring). Nothing observes which\n   arguments reached `resolve_ticket_id`, so the stub's effect is invisible. The call\n   count does not differ either: `resolved_cache` resolves each distinct ref exactly once\n   on both paths.\n2. **material_balding.** The fixture's ticket directory is named `ticket-1`, the same as\n   the ticket id. `_ids.resolve_ticket_id` takes its fast path for an id that names an\n   existing directory (`_ids.py:518-520`) and returns `ticket-1` — exactly what the stub\n   `os.fspath(Path(ticket_id))` returns. The stub and the real resolver agree on this\n   fixture, so removing the stub cannot change the result.\n\n## Change\n\nTest-only. No production code changes. No assertion present today is removed.\n\n1. `test_referencing_commits_falls_back_to_per_call_scan_without_index` — wrap\n   `rebar._ids.resolve_ticket_id` in a recording spy (delegating to the real function)\n   and assert every call received `alias_index=None` and `dir_names=None`, which is the\n   docstring's stated contract for the fallback (\"alias_index/dir_names both None\").\n   `commit_impact` imports `resolve_ticket_id` from `rebar._ids` inside the function\n   body, so the spy is the object it calls. Also assert the spy was called at least once,\n   so the assertion is not vacuous. With the index patch removed, the real index is\n   built and passed, and the new assertion fails.\n2. `test_legacy_payloads_without_material_still_round_trip` — name the fixture's\n   ticket directory differently from the ticket id (for example `legacy-dir` for id\n   `ticket-1`) and make the stub map `ticket-1` to that directory. The stub then decides\n   which directory is read: the real resolver cannot resolve `ticket-1` in that tracker,\n   raises `FileNotFoundError`, both readers return `None`, and the existing equality\n   asserts fail.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] **AC1** For `tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py`, deleting the single\n      `monkeypatch.setattr(..., \"build_resolver_scan_index\", ...)` line in the working tree\n      makes `test_referencing_commits_falls_back_to_per_call_scan_without_index` FAIL;\n      restoring the line makes it pass. The failure assertion is recorded in a ticket\n      comment.\n- [ ] **AC2** For `tests/unit/test_completion_sidecar_material_balding.py`, deleting the\n      single `monkeypatch.setattr(\"rebar._ids.resolve_ticket_dir_name\", ...)` statement\n      makes `test_legacy_payloads_without_material_still_round_trip` FAIL; restoring it\n      makes it pass. The failure assertion is recorded in a ticket comment.\n- [ ] **AC3** Both modules pass unmutated:\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py tests/unit/test_completion_sidecar_material_balding.py -q`\n      exits 0.\n- [ ] **AC4** No assertion present before this change is deleted; `git diff` shows no\n      removed `assert` line in either file.\n- [ ] **AC5** `make lint` exits 0 and `make typecheck` exits 0.\n- [ ] **AC6** The landed commit changes exactly 2 paths:\n      `tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py` and\n      `tests/unit/test_completion_sidecar_material_balding.py`\n      (`git show --name-only --format= <sha>`).\n- [ ] **AC7** The Gerrit change carries `Verified +1` (CI's full `make test` selection)\n      and `LLM-Review +1`, and is merged.\n\n## Out of scope — checked, not merely unexamined\n\n- **The other 5 patch lines** (`test_close_precheck_duplicate_link_c8fd.py:56`,\n  `test_replacement_walker.py:55`, `test_close_duration_instrumentation.py:258,259,464`).\n  All 5 already fail on deletion; measured during db2c, not assumed.\n- **Production resolver behaviour.** The fallback and fast-path behaviour is correct;\n  only the tests' power to detect a missing patch is at fault."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793469977090052001,"uuid":"8920b909-01dc-4f34-9f98-72be81be1d33"}